"944f2e32-83df-59c4-9e62-dddd2a35c6d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125343 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125344 #edb/ref 1061 653148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125344 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "216d2c5a-6567-5c0a-8f69-8218d3f867f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125344 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125345 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125345 #edb/ref 1061 653144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125345 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "da0d1383-fa40-55ef-9635-eb9be7d2ca55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125345 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125346 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125346 #edb/ref 1061 653147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125346 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "307549b1-c36a-5e02-9555-3fe8462e3c33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125346 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125347 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125347 #edb/ref 1061 653141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125347 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e2b92597-3696-53e3-94fd-361b7b47d46c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125347 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125760 #edb/ref 1064 "The set of vectors $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_n \\}$ in an abstract vector space $V$ is **linearly dependent** if there exist real scalars $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ not all zero such that \n\n$$\nx_1\\mathbf{v}_1+x_2\\mathbf{v}_2+\\ldots+x_n\\mathbf{v}_n=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if $x_1 = x_2 = \\cdots = x_n = 0$ is the only solution, the set is **linearly independent**.\n\nLet's consider some examples.\n\n- First, let's consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ of all polynomials in variable $t,$ along with the set\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{ \\underbrace{1}_{\\color{blue}p_1(t)}, \\: \\underbrace{t}_{\\color{blue}p_2(t)}, \\: \\underbrace{t^2}_{\\color{blue}p_3(t)} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo determine whether $S$ is linearly dependent in $\\mathbb{R}[t],$ we must solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}(t) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given expressions for $p_1(t),p_2(t)$ and $p_3(t)$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\cdot 1+{x}_{2}\\cdot t+{x}_{3}\\cdot {t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}t+{x}_{3}{t}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0$ is the only solution, $S$ is linearly independent in $\\mathbb R[t].$\n\n- Let's consider the vector space $\\mathbb R[t]$ once more, along with the set \n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{ \\underbrace{t^2+1}_{\\color{red}q_1(t)}, \\: \\underbrace{-2t^2-2}_{\\color{red}q_2(t)} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo determine whether $T$ is linearly dependent in $\\mathbb{R}[t],$ we must solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{q}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{q}_{2}(t) & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}({t}^{2}+1)+{x}_{2}(-2{t}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ ({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2})+({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has the nonzero solution $(x_1,x_2)=(2,1).$ As a result, we get\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot {\\color{red}q_1(t)} + 1 \\cdot {\\color{red}q_2(t)} = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\big\\{ q_1(t), q_2(t) \\big\\}$ is linearly dependent in $\\mathbb R[t].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125760 #edb/ref 1065 3197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125760 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "939069a1-d4fd-51b0-8c49-c1cdde533481" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125760 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125761 #edb/ref 1064 "Note the following important property of linearly dependent sets.\n\n*A set of vectors is linearly dependent if and only if it contains a vector that is a linear combination of **other** vectors from that set.*\n\nTo see why this is true, let $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2,\\cdots, \\mathbf{v}_n\\}$ be a linearly dependent set of $n$ vectors. This means that the equation\n\n$$\nx_1\\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\cdots + x_n\\mathbf{v}_n = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas a non-trivial solution, i.e. a solution in which at least one of the coefficients $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is not zero. Without loss of generality, we may assume that $x_1 \\ne 0,$ and we can rewrite the above equation as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1 = -\\dfrac{x_2}{x_1} \\mathbf{v}_2 - \\dfrac{x_3}{x_1}\\mathbf{v}_3 - \\cdots - \\dfrac{x_n}{x_1}\\mathbf{v}_n.\n$$\n\nThe above argument shows that the vector $\\mathbf{v}_1$ can be written as a linear combination of the other vectors from the set." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125761 #edb/ref 1065 3198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125761 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7600b92d-52bf-5c9a-bd48-5e9d12eb0d25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125761 #edb/ref 1067 "An Important Property of Linearly Dependent Sets" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241315 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241315 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241315 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0fc3840d-620a-53db-9f16-cb3409201cc2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241482 #edb/ref 1080 "The set is linearly independent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241482 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241482 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "22d74144-a670-5f1c-ab6e-5421e861f28b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241511 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241511 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241511 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "26908c88-4546-5c39-8a0d-31d5ea044633" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242157 #edb/ref 1080 "I 